  </Steps>
  <ThenNow
    title="The same ideas today"
    rows={[
      { then: 'A TextEditor class built on Digi-Test’s window class', now: '<code>class TextEditor extends Window</code> in TypeScript', why: 'The child gets everything the parent has and replaces only what it needs.' },
      { then: 'One hidden field: the address of the class’s method table', now: 'The prototype link behind every JavaScript object', why: 'One hidden pointer per object says where its methods live.' },
    ]}
  />
</section>

<section id="eg-virtual">
  <h2>How a key finds the editor</h2>
  <p class="lead">Digi-Test’s key loop was written before the editor existed. It still hands every key to his new code, with three instructions and a table.</p>
  <CodeStepper id="eg-loop" title="EDIT.EXE  the key loop" cTitle="The same in C++" asm={ASM} c={C} steps={editor}>
    <EgChain slot="visual" editor={editor} plain={plain} />
  </CodeStepper>
  <p>
    This is the heart of object-oriented code, and it is why his reuse worked. The loop only knows “the window in front”
    and asks it to handle the key. The window’s <Term id="vtable">method table</Term> decides which code runs. Put a plain
    Digi-Test window in front, above, and the same three instructions land in Digi-Test’s handler instead. A browser does
    the same today when it hands a click to whatever element you clicked. So does a UI framework when it calls your
    component’s handler. The <Term id="event-loop">event loop</Term> stays the same, and each kind of window brings its own
    behavior.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="eg-insert">
  <h2>Type into his line</h2>
  <p class="lead">Every line of text has its own block of 255 bytes. Type below, and his insert routine runs on every key.</p>
  <EgType />
  <div class="eg-cols">
    <p>
      The editor keeps a table of 500 line slots, 2,000 bytes in all. A line gets its block only when you first type on
      it, so an empty file costs almost nothing. Insert is the in-place version of <code>s.slice(0, i) + key + s.slice(i)</code>
      in JavaScript. His <code>movmem</code> slides the rest of the line to the right inside its own block, and the key
      drops into the gap. No other line is ever touched.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <b>Past the end</b>, then type. EDIT lets the cursor stand anywhere on the line and fills the gap with spaces
      when a key arrives, a feature editors still call virtual space. Switch to <b>Overwrite</b> and a key replaces the
      byte under the cursor, while a Tab still opens 8 fresh spaces.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Deep title="For engineers: the insert routine">
    <Listing
      title="EDIT.EXE 02D1:0D16, TextEditor insert"
      lines={[
        { off: '0D16', code: 'cmp  byte ptr [bp+0Ah],9', note: 'is the key a Tab?' },
        { off: '0D1F', code: 'mov  si,es:[bx+3Ah]', note: 'yes: width = the tab width, 8' },
        { off: '0D23', code: 'mov  byte ptr [bp+0Ah],20h', note: 'and write spaces' },
        { off: '0D29', code: 'mov  si,1', note: 'a letter: width 1' },
        { off: '0D2F', code: 'mov  ax,es:[bx+38h]', note: 'the cursor column' },
        { off: '0D36', code: 'cmp  ax,0FFh', note: 'column + width - 1 inside the 255 bytes?' },
        { off: '0D68', code: 'cmp  es:[bx+38h],di', note: 'cursor past the end of the text?' },
        { off: '0D83', code: 'call far 0000:1D22', note: 'memset(line + len, 20h, col - len): fill the gap', hi: true },
        { off: '0D8E', code: 'cmp  word ptr es:[bx+42h],0', note: 'insert mode?' },
        { off: '0DC3', code: 'call far 0000:1D41', note: 'movmem(line + col, line + col + n, 255 - col)', hi: true },
        { off: '0DE0', code: 'call far 0000:1D22', note: 'memset(line + col, ch, n): the key', hi: true },
      ]}
      foot="1D22h and 1D41h are memset and movmem in Borland’s library. The arguments go on the stack before each call; the notes show them in C."
    />
  </Deep>
</section>

